#doc (Manual) "Elimination of indeterminacy after Deligne and Conrad" =>

:::group "grp:conrad"
Extension of a partial section after an admissible blow-up, along the route of Deligne and
Conrad: the disjointness blow-up, the three cases of a target quasi-affine over the base,
the quasi-finiteness of the closed graph, and the general theorem obtained from Zariski's
Main Theorem.
:::

Throughout, $`X` is a quasi-compact quasi-separated scheme and $`U \le V` are quasi-compact
opens of $`X`. We study the problem of extending a _partial section_: a morphism
$`f \colon U \to Y` with $`p \circ f = \iota_U` for a morphism $`p \colon Y \to X`. The aim
is to extend $`f`, after an admissible blow-up of the base, to a section over an open
neighbourhood of the preserved open, with _closed graph_ in the fibre product.

# Deligne's disjointness blow-up

:::theorem "thm:disjointness" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.disjoint_closure_range_of_blowupMap_sup, AlgebraicGeometry.disjoint_support_ker_of_blowupMap_sup")
Let $`p \colon Z \to X` be a morphism, let $`f \colon Y \to Z` be a morphism over $`X` whose
source lies over an open $`U \le V` of $`X`, and let $`\sI_1, \sI_2` be quasi-coherent ideal
sheaves on $`X` such that

* $`V \setminus U \subseteq V(\sI_1)` as sets, so that $`T_1 = V(\sI_1^{2})` is a square-zero
  thickening of a closed subscheme containing the boundary of $`U` inside $`V`;
* $`V(\sI_2) \subseteq X \setminus V` as sets;
* the closed subscheme $`p^{-1}T_1 \cap \overline{Y}` of $`Z` factors through $`p^{-1}T_2`,
  where $`\overline{Y}` is the scheme-theoretic image of $`f`; in ideal-sheaf language,
  $`\sI_2 \cdot \sO_Z \le (\sI_1^{2}) \cdot \sO_Z \sqcup \ker f`.

Blow up $`\sI_1 \sqcup \sI_2` {uses "def:separation-blowup"}[] and base change everything
along the blow-up morphism $`q`. Then the scheme-theoretic image of the base change of $`f`
no longer meets the part of the base change of $`Z` lying over
$`\overline{q^{-1}(V \setminus U)}`.
:::

:::proof "thm:disjointness" (uses := "thm:0806, def:blowup")
The charts of $`\Bl_{\sI_1 \sqcup \sI_2}(X)` at sections of $`\sI_1` are harmless: there the
inverse images of $`\sI_1` and of $`\sI_1 \sqcup \sI_2` have the same support, so a point of
such a chart lying over $`V(\sI_1)` lies over $`V(\sI_2)` as well, which is disjoint from
$`V`; hence such a chart misses $`q^{-1}(V \setminus U)` altogether, and therefore also its
closure. Only the charts at sections $`s_2` of $`\sI_2` remain. On an affine probe of the
base change lying over such a chart, the extended centre is generated by the image $`\sigma`
of $`s_2`, so the factorization hypothesis reads $`\sigma \in \sigma a_1 + \mathfrak{n}`,
with $`a_1` the extension of $`\sI_1` and $`\mathfrak{n}` the kernel of the base change of
$`f`; that is, $`\sigma(1 - h) \in \mathfrak{n}` with $`h \in a_1`. Since the source of $`f`
lies over $`U`, which misses $`V(\sI_1 \sqcup \sI_2)`, the extension of the centre there is
the unit ideal, so multiplying by $`\sigma` does not change the kernel and
$`1 - h \in \mathfrak{n}`. Then $`1 = h + (1 - h)` shows that the inverse images of $`\sI_1`
and the kernel generate the unit ideal on the probe, so their supports do not meet.
:::

# The case of a target that is quasi-affine over the base

:::theorem "thm:conrad-case1-affine-space" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_affineSpace, AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup.awaySlope, AlgebraicGeometry.AffineSpace.isClosed_range_of_basicOpen_fractions")
Let $`X` be affine, let $`U \subseteq X` be a quasi-compact open, let $`\sigma` be finite and
let $`f \colon U \to \Aff(\sigma; X)` be a morphism over $`X`. Then there are a
$`U`-admissible centre $`\sI` on $`X`, an open $`W \subseteq \Bl_\sI(X)` containing
$`\pi_\sI^{-1}U` and a morphism $`g \colon W \to \Aff(\sigma; X)` over $`X` which agrees with
$`f` over $`\pi_\sI^{-1}U`, such that the graph
$`W \to \Bl_\sI(X) \times_X \Aff(\sigma; X)` has closed range.
:::

:::proof "thm:conrad-case1-affine-space" (uses := "def:admissible, def:affine-blowup, def:blowup")
Cover $`U` by finitely many basic opens $`D(u)`. On $`D(u)` the coordinates of $`f` are
fractions $`g_{u,j}/u^{N}` for a single large $`N`, and after one further enlargement of
$`N` the compatibility identities $`g_{u,j}\,u'^{\,N} = g_{u',j}\,u^{N}` hold exactly in the
global sections. Let $`\sI` be generated by all $`u^{N}` and all $`g_{u,j}`; it restricts to
the unit ideal on $`U`, hence is $`U`-admissible. On the blow-up chart at $`u^{N}` the
$`j`-th coordinate extends as the degree-one fraction
$`(g_{u,j} \cdot x)/(u^{N} \cdot x)`, and the exact identities make the chart transitions
hold on the nose, so the local extensions glue over the union $`W` of the charts at the
$`u^{N}`, which contains $`\pi_\sI^{-1}U`. Closedness of the graph is checked on the chart
at each generator $`a` of $`\sI`: there the graph is the vanishing locus of the sections
built from the fractions $`(u_i^{N}x)/(ax)` and $`(g_{u_i,j}x)/(ax)`, because these
fractions generate the unit ideal on the chart.
:::

:::theorem "thm:conrad-case1" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isQuasiAffine, AlgebraicGeometry.exists_extend_of_lift")
Let $`X` be affine, let $`U \subseteq X` be a quasi-compact open, let $`Y` be a quasi-affine
scheme and let $`p \colon Y \to X` be locally of finite type. Let $`f \colon U \to Y` satisfy
$`p \circ f = \iota_U`. Then there are a $`U`-admissible centre $`\sI` on $`X`, an open
$`W \subseteq \Bl_\sI(X)` containing $`\pi_\sI^{-1}U` and a morphism $`g \colon W \to Y` with
$`p \circ g = \pi_\sI|_W`, agreeing with $`f` over $`\pi_\sI^{-1}U`, such that the graph
$`W \to Y \times_X \Bl_\sI(X)` has closed range and $`\pi_\sI^{-1}U` is
scheme-theoretically dense in $`\Bl_\sI(X)`.
:::

:::proof "thm:conrad-case1" (uses := "thm:04II, thm:conrad-case1-affine-space, thm:dense-admissible-exists")
Embed $`Y` into a relative affine space over $`X`, extend $`f` composed with that immersion,
and then compose with one further admissible blow-up making the preserved open schematically
dense, transporting the extension along the comparison of the two blow-ups.
:::

:::theorem "thm:conrad-case2" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isAffineOpen, AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isQuasiAffineHom")
Let $`X` be quasi-compact quasi-separated, let $`U \subseteq X` be a quasi-compact open, and
let $`p \colon Y \to X` be separated, locally of finite type and quasi-affine as a morphism.
Let $`f \colon U \to Y` satisfy $`p \circ f = \iota_U`. Then the conclusion of the previous
theorem holds verbatim, with $`U = V`.
:::

:::proof "thm:conrad-case2" (uses := "thm:conrad-case1, thm:01PF, thm:01PH, thm:admissible-density")
Apply Case 1 over each member of a finite affine open cover of $`X`, extend the local centres
found there to the whole base, and blow up the product of the extensions times a finite-type
ideal cutting out $`X \setminus U`. The support of the resulting centre is exactly
$`X \setminus U`, so the inverse image of $`U` is scheme-theoretically dense and in
particular topologically dense. That density makes the local extension loci pairwise
compatible, which in turn makes the graph of the glued extension meet the part of
$`Y \times_X X'` over each member of the cover in the closed local graph there.
:::

:::theorem "thm:conrad-case3" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isQuasiAffineHom_of_le, AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_disjoint_closure_range_of_le, PrespectralSpace.exists_isCompact_isOpen_disjoint_union_eq_univ")
Let $`X` be quasi-compact quasi-separated, let $`U \le V` be quasi-compact opens of $`X`, let
$`Y` be quasi-compact quasi-separated and let $`p \colon Y \to X` be separated, locally of
finite type and quasi-affine as a morphism. Let $`f \colon U \to Y` satisfy
$`p \circ f = \iota_U` and assume the range of $`f` is closed in $`p^{-1}V`. Then there are a
$`V`-admissible centre $`\sI` on $`X`, an open $`W \subseteq \Bl_\sI(X)` containing
$`\pi_\sI^{-1}U`, and a morphism $`g \colon W \to Y` with $`p \circ g = \pi_\sI|_W` agreeing
with $`f` over $`\pi_\sI^{-1}U`, whose graph $`W \to Y \times_X \Bl_\sI(X)` has closed range.
Density of $`U` in $`X` is not assumed.
:::

:::proof "thm:conrad-case3" (uses := "thm:disjointness, thm:finite-type-directed, lem:power-in-ideal, thm:conrad-case2, thm:080M, cor:080L")
Two opening reductions bring the situation to Case 2. First, one $`V`-admissible blow-up
$`\pi_1 \colon X_1 \to X` produced by the disjointness blow-up makes the closure of the image
of the base change of $`f` disjoint from the part lying over $`\overline{V \setminus U}`;
here the two centres are a finite-type subideal $`\sI_1` of the vanishing ideal of the
reduced $`\overline{V \setminus U}`, supplied by compactness of finite-type ideal sheaves,
and $`\sI_2 = \sI_1^{2} \sqcup \sK^{n}`, where $`\sK` cuts out $`X \setminus V` and $`n` is
large enough by the power lemma. Second, since the carrier of a scheme is prespectral, a
quasi-compact subset of an open is contained in a quasi-compact open of it; this yields a
quasi-compact open $`X_0 \subseteq X_1` containing that closure's image and meeting
$`\pi_1^{-1}V` exactly in $`\pi_1^{-1}U`. Over $`X_0` the two opens coincide, so Case 2
applies to the base change of $`p`; the centre found over $`X_0` is extended to $`X_1` by the
extension theorem for admissible centres, whose hypothesis is exactly
$`X_0 \cap \pi_1^{-1}V = \pi_1^{-1}U`, and the two admissible blow-ups are composed.
:::

# The general case

:::theorem "thm:graph-quasi-finite" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_locallyQuasiFinite_image_graph, AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_of_isAffineOpen_of_le, AlgebraicGeometry.isClosed_preimage_range_lift_of_comp")
Let $`X` be quasi-compact quasi-separated, let $`U \le V` be quasi-compact opens, let $`Y` be
quasi-compact quasi-separated and let $`p \colon Y \to X` be separated and locally of finite
type. Let $`f \colon U \to Y` satisfy $`p \circ f = \iota_U` and have range closed in
$`p^{-1}V`. Then there are a $`V`-admissible centre $`\sI` on $`X` and an open immersion
$`\sigma \colon U \to \Bl_\sI(X)` lifting $`\iota_U`, such that for the graph
$`F = (f, \sigma) \colon U \to Y \times_X \Bl_\sI(X)` the scheme-theoretic image
$`\operatorname{im} F` is quasi-finite, of finite type, separated and quasi-compact over
$`\Bl_\sI(X)`.
:::

:::proof "thm:graph-quasi-finite" (uses := "thm:conrad-case3, thm:0806, cor:080L")
Cover $`Y` by finitely many affine opens $`Z`. For each of them the composite
$`Z \to Y \to X` is quasi-affine as a morphism, so Case 3 applies to the restriction of $`f`
to $`f^{-1}Z` and produces a $`V`-admissible centre $`\sI_Z` on $`X` together with an open
$`W_Z` of $`\Bl_{\sI_Z}(X)` carrying an extension of $`f|_{f^{-1}Z}` with closed graph in
$`Z \times_X \Bl_{\sI_Z}(X)`. Let $`\sI = \prod_Z \sI_Z`. Each $`\sI_Z` pulls back to an
invertible ideal on $`\Bl_\sI(X)`, so the universal property provides comparison morphisms
$`\Bl_\sI(X) \to \Bl_{\sI_Z}(X)` over $`X`, and the closed graph of each local extension
pulls back to a closed subset of the open $`\mathrm{pr}_Y^{-1}Z` of
$`Y \times_X \Bl_\sI(X)` which is a section over an open of $`\Bl_\sI(X)`. These opens cover
$`Y \times_X \Bl_\sI(X)`, and the closure of the range of $`F` meets each of them inside the
corresponding section; hence the fibres of $`\operatorname{im} F \to \Bl_\sI(X)` are finite.
:::

:::definition "def:quasi-affine-hom" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.IsQuasiAffineHom, AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.exists_isOpenImmersion_isAffineHom_of_locallyQuasiFinite, AlgebraicGeometry.IsQuasiAffineHom.of_locallyQuasiFinite, AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IsQuasiAffine.of_locallyQuasiFinite, AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IsQuasiAffine.of_isQuasiAffineHom, AlgebraicGeometry.IsQuasiAffineHom.of_isPullback")
A morphism is _quasi-affine_ if the preimage of every affine open of the target is a
quasi-affine scheme. The two consequences of Zariski's Main Theorem used below are recorded
here: a separated, quasi-compact, locally quasi-finite morphism of finite type factors as an
open immersion followed by an affine morphism; consequently such a morphism is quasi-affine
as a morphism, with no hypothesis on the target, and its source is a quasi-affine scheme
whenever the target is. Zariski's Main Theorem itself is taken from the ambient library.
:::

:::theorem "thm:conrad24" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_admissibleBlowup_extend_partialSection, AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_partialSection_of_blowup, AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissible_extend_partialSection_of_isClosedImmersion, AlgebraicGeometry.baseChangeSection")
Let $`X` be quasi-compact quasi-separated, let $`U \le V` be quasi-compact opens of $`X` with
$`U` dense in $`X`, let $`Y` be quasi-compact quasi-separated and let $`p \colon Y \to X` be
separated, quasi-compact and locally of finite type. Let $`f \colon U \to Y` satisfy
$`p \circ f = \iota_U` and have range closed in $`p^{-1}V`. Then there are a $`V`-admissible
centre $`\sI` on $`X`, an open $`W \subseteq \Bl_\sI(X)` containing $`\pi_\sI^{-1}U` and a
morphism $`g \colon W \to Y` with $`p \circ g = \pi_\sI|_W` which agrees with $`f` over
$`\pi_\sI^{-1}U`, such that the graph $`W \to Y \times_X \Bl_\sI(X)` has closed range.
:::

:::proof "thm:conrad24" (uses := "thm:conrad-case3, thm:graph-quasi-finite, def:quasi-affine-hom, cor:080L")
If $`p` is quasi-affine as a morphism this is Case 3. In general the quasi-finiteness of the
closed graph produces a $`V`-admissible blow-up $`q \colon \widetilde{X} \to X` over which
the scheme-theoretic image of the graph is quasi-finite, of finite type, separated and
quasi-compact over $`\widetilde{X}`; Zariski's Main Theorem turns these four properties into
quasi-affineness of $`\operatorname{im} F \to \widetilde{X}` _as a morphism_ (neither
$`\widetilde{X}` nor $`\operatorname{im} F` need be quasi-affine as a scheme). Two purely
formal reductions finish the proof: one may base change the whole situation along a
$`V`-admissible blow-up without leaving the statement, the blow-up produced downstairs being
the composite of two admissible blow-ups; and one may replace the target by a closed
subscheme through which the section factors, here by the scheme-theoretic image of $`U`.
After these two reductions the target is quasi-affine over the base and Case 3 applies.
:::

:::corollary "cor:conrad25" (parent := "grp:conrad") (lean := "AlgebraicGeometry.exists_admissibleBlowup_extend_section")
Let $`X` be quasi-compact quasi-separated, let $`U \subseteq X` be a dense quasi-compact
open, let $`Y` be quasi-compact quasi-separated and let $`p \colon Y \to X` be separated,
quasi-compact and locally of finite type. Then any $`f \colon U \to Y` with
$`p \circ f = \iota_U` extends, after a $`U`-admissible blow-up, to a section over an open of
the blow-up containing the preimage of $`U`, with closed graph.
:::

:::proof "cor:conrad25" (uses := "thm:conrad24")
No closedness hypothesis is needed: over $`U` the morphism $`f` is a section of the separated
morphism $`p|_U`, hence a closed immersion, so its range is closed in $`p^{-1}U`.
:::
